  "major": "applied-psychology-ba-nyc-56cf4a31",
  "major_name": "Applied Psychology",
  "degree": "BA",
  "campus": "New York",
  "college": "Bouvé College of Health Sciences",
  "overview_text": "The Bachelor of Arts in Applied Psychology provides students with an understanding of the biological, behavioral, emotional, and cognitive factors influencing human health and well-being. Students develop writing and communications skills and apply research methods and analytic techniques to advance their own research. The program is a series of courses centered on developing students' understanding of the determinants of behavioral health, theories and practices related to ameliorating mental health problems, and promoting health and wellness. Each student is required to complete a core course sequence including courses in biology, basic and applied psychology, and health sciences. Academic Policies and Procedures Please visit Bouvé College of Health Sciences Undergraduate page for academic policies and procedures, including Academic Appeals . Please visit Bouvé College Learning Outcomes for the specific student learning outcomes for this program. This program is currently only available to former Marymount Manhattan College students who are eligible for continued enrollment at Northeastern University. This program includes required courses that will be taught on ground at the Northeastern New York City campus.",
  "source_text": "Complete all courses listed below unless otherwise indicated. Also complete any corequisite labs, recitations, clinicals, or tools courses where specified and complete any additional courses needed beyond specific college and major requirements to satisfy graduation credit requirements.\nUniversitywide Requirements\nAll undergraduate students are required to complete the\nUniversitywide Requirements\n.\nBA Language Requirements\nAll BA students are required to complete the BA degree language requirements, for a total of 12 semester hours of language study or demonstrated equivalent proficiency, as described in\nAdditional Requirements for BA students\n. Successful demonstration of proficiency does not reduce total minimum semester hours of study required to earn the BA degree.\nNUpath Requirements\nAll undergraduate students are required to complete the\nNUpath Requirements\n.\nNew York State Liberal Arts and Sciences Requirements\nAll undergraduate students enrolled in programs at the New York campus are required to fulfill\nNew York State Liberal Arts and Sciences requirements\n.\nCore Requirements\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nCAEP 2012\nHealth Psychology: An Introduction\n4\nCAEP 2106\nHistory and Systems of Psychology\n4\nCAEP 4525\nIntroduction to Professional Psychology\n4\nHLTH 2100\nInterprofessional Ethics for Individual and Population Health\n4\nHLTH 5410\nIntroduction to Statistics in Health and Behavioral Science\n3\nor\nPHTH 2210\nFoundations of Biostatistics\nHLTH 5450\nHealthcare Research\n4\nPSYC 1101\nFoundations of Psychology\n4\nPSYC 3404\nDevelopmental Psychology\n4\nPSYC 3406\nClinical Psychology and Mental Health\n4\nPSYC 3450\nLearning and Motivation\n4\nPSYC 3466\nCognition\n4\nCapstone Requirement\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nHSCI 4740\nHealth Science Capstone Seminar\n4\nScience Requirements\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nBiology Options\nComplete one of the following options:\n8-10\nOption 1\nBIOL 1143\nBiology and Society\nor\nBIOL 1141\nMicrobes and Society\nBIOL 1147\nThe Human Organism\nOption 2\nBIOL 1111\nand\nBIOL 1112\nGeneral Biology 1\nand Lab for BIOL 1111\nBIOL 1113\nand\nBIOL 1114\nGeneral Biology 2\nand Lab for BIOL 1113\nMajor Electives\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nComplete two of the following:\n8\nCAEP 1280\nIntroduction to Mindfulness\nCAEP 1290\nPersonal Behavior Change\nCAEP 2101\nBehavioral Assessment and Treatment of Health Problems in the 21st Century\nCAEP 2105\nCollege Student Mental Health\nCAEP 2290\nThe Yoga Tradition: Philosophy, Methods, and Practice\nCAEP 3200\nChildhood Adversity and College Attainment\nCAEP 3310\nSay It Loud: The Black Power Movement and Higher Education\nCAEP 3480\nCounseling Theories and Practice\nCAEP 3485\nMental Health and Counseling\nCAEP 5330\nApplied Animal Behavior Analysis\nPSYC 3400\nPersonality\nPSYC 3402\nSocial Psychology\nMajor Credit Requirement\nComplete 63 semester hours in the major.\nOther Major Requirements\nCourse List\nCode\nTitle\nHours\nIntroduction to College\nHSCI 1000\nCollege: An Introduction\n1\nor\nHLTH 1000\nCollege: An Introduction\nWriting Courses\nENGW 1111\nFirst-Year Writing\n4\nENGW 3306\nAdvanced Writing in the Health Professions\n4\nExperiential Learning\nSelect one of the following options:\n1-4\nOption 1: Co-op\nStudents may choose to complete one or multiple six-month co-op experiences during their course of study.  Students must consult with their advisor and complete\nEXED 2000\nif participating in co-op.\nEXED 2000\nis only required once.\nEXED 2000\nProfessional Development for Co-op\n1\nCOOP 3945\nCo-op Work Experience\nor\nCOOP 3946\nCo-op Work Experience--Half Time\nor\nCOOP 3947\nCo-op Work Experience Abroad--Half Time\nor\nCOOP 3948\nCo-op Work Experience Abroad\nOption 2: Course Approved for NUpath Integrating Knowledge and Skills Through Experience\nAny course(s) designated with NUpath attribute EX, including courses used to fulfill other requirements of this program.\nApplied Psychology Major Requirements\nFor students seeking to apply CAEP 5000-CAEP 6999 courses to a PlusOne pathway to a master's program, a B or higher is required.\nStudents will also be required to satisfy the\nuniversity’s Additional Requirements for BA Students\nas outlined in the catalog.\nStudents are responsible for using electives in this program to complete NUpath requirements not satisfied by required courses in this program.\nProgram Requirements\nMinimum of 128 semester hours required",
